ans the query forces**\n\n1. **Full scan of `users`.** `lower(u.email) = '...'` wraps the column in a function, so a plain index on `email` can't be used. The database reads every user row and runs `lower()` on each one. This is the most likely culprit, and it gets slower as the user table grows.\n2. **Scan of `orders` on the join key.** The join is on `o.user_id = u.id`. Without an index on `orders.user_id`, the database either scans all of `orders` (a hash join) or scans it once per matching user (a nested loop). If an index on that column was dropped or never existed, this would explain a sudden regression. Check migrations from last week.\n3. **Wide row fetch from `SELECT *`.** This pulls every column of both tables, including any large text or JSON columns. It also rules out index-only scans. It adds cost but isn't the main cause.\n\n**Fixes (Postgres syntax)**\n\n```sql\n-- 1. Make the lookup sargable (UNIQUE if emails are unique case-insensitively)\nCREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_users_email_lower ON users (lower(email));\n\n-- 2. Index the join/filter column on orders\nCREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_orders_user_id ON orders (user_id);\n```\n\nThe expression in the index has to match the query exactly, so `lower(email)` in both places.\n\nA cleaner long-term option is to store emails normalized (lowercased on write, or use a `citext` column). Then the query becomes `WHERE u.email = ...` and an ordinary unique index covers it.\n\nThen trim the select list to the columns the page actually renders:\n\n```sql\nSELECT o.id, o.created_at, o.total, o.status   -- whatever the page needs\nFROM orders o\nJOIN users u ON u.id = o.user_id\nWHERE lower(u.email) = 'a@example.org'\nORDER BY o.created_at DESC\nLIMIT 50;\n```\n\nIf the page lists orders, add `ORDER BY` and `LIMIT`. An index on `orders (user_id, created_at DESC)` would then serve both the join and the sort.\n\n**How to verify**\n\nRun `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` before and after. Before, expect a `Seq Scan on users` and possibly one on `orders`. After, expect index scans on both. If it's still slow, send me the plan, the table definitions and the row counts, and I can narrow it down. I haven't changed any files.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}